abstract: "For every pair of complementary (mutually unbiased) qutrit bases and every two orthogonal qutrit states, the triangular discrimination summed over the two bases is at most 8/3, with every equality case classified (tied to the Hesse SIC rays for the computational and Fourier bases); proof by an exact rational sum-of-squares certificate checked by a standalone verifier. Consequences: a 2/3 cap on the Fisher-information efficiency of two random complementary settings and a CQC margin of at least 0.1005 nats for Qian's two-ray qubit-qutrit family. For a qubit with a qudit of dimension n >= 4, every prescribed pair of complementary measurements admits a separable state violating the complementary quantum correlation (CQC) inequality, with a uniform certified margin below -9/20000000 nats (previously known only for n >= 164); a related construction covers both dimensions at least three. The full qubit-qutrit CQC question remains open. Unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only. Text CC0-1.0; code MIT."
